  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does an electric eel fire its shock?",
      "a": "Its brain sends a signal down nerves that reach every electrocyte at the same moment. All the cells pump charged particles at once, and their small voltages add up like batteries in a row.",
      "asks": [
        "What controls an electric eel's shock?",
        "How do all the cells fire together?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What are electrocytes?",
      "a": "Electrocytes are flat, disc-shaped cells that evolved from muscle cells. Each makes about 0.15 volts. Stacked by the thousand in series, they make hundreds of volts, and many rows side by side give a strong current.",
      "asks": [
        "What is an electric organ made of?",
        "How can cells make electricity?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What are the weak electric pulses for?",
      "a": "The Sachs' organ sends low-voltage pulses that make an electric field around the eel. Objects and animals distort the field, so the eel can sense them in dark, muddy rivers.",
      "asks": [
        "Can electric eels sense with electricity?",
        "What is Sachs' organ?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How strong is an electric eel's shock?",
      "a": "Typical shocks are several hundred volts, and Volta's electric eel, described in 2019, can reach 860 volts, the strongest of any animal. The shock lasts only milliseconds, but it can stun fish and hurt a person.",
      "asks": [
        "Can an electric eel kill a person?",
        "How many volts can an electric eel make?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Is an electric eel really an eel?",
      "a": "No. Electric eels are knifefish, more closely related to catfish and carp than to true eels. They also breathe air, coming up to gulp it every few minutes.",
      "asks": [
        "What kind of fish is an electric eel?",
        "Do electric eels breathe air?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Volta's electric eel, described in 2019, can discharge up to 860 volts, the highest of any known animal.",
